88:           "policy": "Each fisher may take up to 2 units of fish per day; any excess must be left in the lake or deposited into a communal reserve, which is locked in the communal shed and accessed only when total lake stock falls below the community\u2019s daily survival need (number of villagers \u00d7 1 unit).",
89:           "operationalization": "1. Daily: every fisher records their catch on the shared ledger board; the ledger entry is made in the presence of at least one other fisher. 2. Exceeding 2 units: if a fisher logs more than 2 units, the excess must be immediately dropped back into the lake or placed in the reserve container; the ledger must note the amount of excess. 3. Reserve container: sits in the communal storage shed, locked with a padlock. 4. Lock\u2011keepers: a rotating trio of trusted fishers holds the padlock keys; the trio is elected monthly at the last\u2011day community meeting by drawing lots (each adult fisher draws a number; the three highest become lock\u2011keepers). 5. Reserve access: the reserve may only be opened when a community meeting determines that the remaining fish in the lake (after all catches) is less than the daily survival need. 6. Audit: the ledger is audited weekly; any discrepancies trigger the fisher to return excess fish to the lake or deposit it in the reserve, and may result in a temporary one\u2011day fishing ban. 7. Reserve use: upon opening, the reserve is divided equally among fishers who lack sufficient catch for the day; deposits and withdrawals are recorded by the person making the action. 8. Transparency: all ledger entries, audits, reserve changes, and rotation decisions are publicly posted on the shared board and recorded in the community ledger. 9. Enforcement: peer monitoring, public ledger audit, and temporary fishing restriction provide social sanction if rules are broken.",
90:           "reasoning": "This version keeps the core of the proposal\u2014max 2 units per fisher, reserve use only when needed, transparent ledger, rotating lock\u2011keepers\u2014while adding explicit timelines for rotation, audit, and enforcement to promote sustainability, fairness, and accountability for future generations.",
