101:           "operationalization": "1. Tally keeper: chosen by majority vote each rule proposal, serves one month, rotates thereafter, keeps the communal tally sheet and a 30\u2011cm measuring stick marked in centimeters. 2. Fishing excursion: a fisher\u2019s day starts when they leave the dock and ends when they return or when the sun sets; they may keep up to three fish 10 cm or longer, after which the tally keeper stops the log for that day. 3. Recording: upon return, the fisher places each fish 10 cm or longer on the tally sheet, noting size and date; fish below 10 cm are released immediately. 4. Verification: the tally keeper measures each fish with the stick, confirms the size, counts the fish, and ensures no more than three are recorded; any fish beyond three is noted but not counted. 5. Violations: if a fisher records more than three fish or fails to release fish under 10 cm, the tally keeper records a violation. 6. Enforcement: the fisher receives a 3\u2011day temporary ban, cannot bring a net or line for that period, and must sign or verbally acknowledge the rule on the tally sheet before the next trip. 7. Acknowledgment: the fisher signs a confirmation line or verbally states they understand the rule to the tally keeper; only after acknowledgment may the ban be lifted. 8. Community review: the policy and enforcement procedures are reviewed and can be amended by majority vote, with the tally keeper role rotating to maintain fairness.",
102:           "reasoning": "This version consolidates all discussion points\u2014tally keeper duties, measuring instrument, fish size release, daily limit, log stopping, violation handling, ban duration, acknowledgment method, and role rotation\u2014into a clear, actionable policy that balances individual autonomy with communal stewardship.",
