107:           "operationalization": "1. A unit of fish is defined as the weight of an average fish of the main catch, agreed upon by the council using a communal weigh\u2011scale; the definition may be amended only with a unanimous community vote.\n2. Each fisher records total hours fished and cumulative catch on the communal sheet, submitting it within one hour of returning to shore.\n3. On stopping, compute family_need = number_of_family_members \u00d7 1 unit; max_allowed_catch = family_need + 2 units.\n4. If cumulative catch > max_allowed_catch, calculate excess_over_limit = cumulative_catch \u2013 max_allowed_catch; return that excess to the community pool and impose a fine of 10% of excess_over_limit (in fish units). If the fisher cannot return the excess in fish, they may pay the fine in silver at a rate of 1 coin per unit of fish owed.\n5. If total hours > 4, calculate excess_after_fourth_hour = hours \u2013 4; any fish caught beyond the fourth hour is considered excess, returned to the pool, and fined at 10% of that excess.\n6. The council verifies the fisher\u2019s log against the fish count, ensuring the returned amount matches the reported excess; discrepancies must be corrected or paid in silver.\n7. Until the fine is paid in full (fish or silver), the fisher forfeits the right to fish the next day; the council may also withhold communal gear or benefits until the debt is cleared.\n8. The council tallies all returned fish and silver payments for community maintenance, gear upkeep, and other shared expenses.\n9. All procedures are recorded on the communal sheet and made available to every villager for transparency.",
108:           "reasoning": "This concise policy balances individual family needs with collective sustainability, sets clear limits on effort and catch, and establishes transparent accountability and enforcement mechanisms overseen by the council.",
109:           "participated": true
