121:             "question": "Who holds the reserve container and how is it secured?",
122:             "answer": "The reserve container will sit in our communal storage shed, locked with a simple padlock. The keys will be held by a rotating trio of trusted fishers chosen by a village vote, so no single person controls it. Every deposit is recorded in the ledger on the shared board so the whole community can see what\u2019s in the reserve. That way the reserve stays safe and available for the next time we need it.",
123:             "revised_policy": "Each fisher may take up to 2 units of fish per day; any catch beyond that must be left in the lake or deposited into a communal reserve for future use.",
124:             "revised_operationalization": "We keep a simple ledger on a shared board; every fisher writes the number of units taken each day. If a fisher exceeds 2 units, the excess must be dropped back into the water or put in the reserve container. The reserve is checked only when fish supplies fall below the daily survival need of the community. The reserve container sits in the communal storage shed, locked with a padlock, and the keys are held by a rotating trio of trusted fishers. The container is marked with the village seal and every deposit is recorded in the ledger."
