127:             "question": "How is the community\u2019s daily survival need defined, and who decides when the reserve is checked and accessed for use?",
128:             "answer": "The village\u2019s daily survival need is simply the number of people who live on the lake times one unit of fish per person, because every villager must eat one unit each day to stay alive.  We decide to look at the reserve only when the total fish left in the lake\u2014after all catch is taken\u2014falls below that amount.  The decision is made by the community as a whole, but in practice it is the rotating trio of trusted fishers who hold the padlock\u2019s keys.  They will open the container only when a village meeting confirms that the lake\u2019s stock is below the survival threshold, and then will distribute the reserve fairly among those who need it.  This keeps the rule simple, prevents over\u2011fishing, and protects future generations.\n\nMy proposal, as originally stated, remains unchanged:\n\n\"Each fisher may take up to 2 units of fish per day; any catch beyond that must be left in the lake or deposited into a communal reserve for future use.  We keep a simple ledger on a shared board; every fisher writes the number of units taken each day.  If a fisher exceeds 2 units, the excess must be dropped back into the water or put in the reserve container.  The reserve is checked only when fish supplies fall below the daily survival need of the community.  The reserve container sits in the communal storage shed, locked with a padlock, and the keys are held by a rotating trio of trusted fishers.  The container is marked with the village seal and every deposit is recorded in the ledger.\"",
129:             "revised_policy": "\"Each fisher may take up to 2 units of fish per day; any catch beyond that must be left in the lake or deposited into a communal reserve for future use.  We keep a simple ledger on a shared board; every fisher writes the number of units taken each day.  If a fisher exceeds 2 units, the excess must be dropped back into the water or put in the reserve container.  The reserve is checked only when fish supplies fall below the daily survival need of the community, which is the number of villagers times 1 unit of fish.  The reserve container sits in the communal storage shed, locked with a padlock, and the keys are held by a rotating trio of trusted fishers.  The container is marked with the village seal and every deposit is recorded in the ledger.\"",
130:             "revised_operationalization": "The reserve is checked when a community meeting determines that the total fish remaining in the lake is less than the village\u2019s daily survival need.  The decision is made by consensus, but the rotating trio of trusted fishers holds the padlock keys and is responsible for opening the container.  Once opened, the reserve is divided equitably among those who lack sufficient catch for the day.  All actions are logged in the ledger, and any new deposit or withdrawal is recorded by the person who made it.  This process ensures transparency and fairness while preserving fish for future use."
