147:             "question": "The proposal doesn't say who holds the communal pot, how its contents are tracked, or how the surplus fish are allocated to help those who catch less.",
148:             "answer": "We will keep the pot in a shared box at the landing, track it with a ledger on the dock, and give the surplus equally to those who didn't reach 1 unit. The pot is managed by the village council with a rotating keeper.",
149:             "revised_policy": "Everyone should fish once per day and aim to catch at least 1 unit of fish. If the catch is less than 1 unit, the fisher will go back for a second trip until they reach 1 unit. Any extra weight beyond 1 unit is set aside in a communal pot, which is kept at the communal landing. The pot is tracked by a simple ledger board where each fisher records the surplus weight. At the end of each day, the surplus fish are divided equally among the fishers who caught less than 1 unit that day. Those who caught exactly 1 unit keep all of their catch. The communal pot is managed by the village council, with a rotating keeper each week.",
150:             "revised_operationalization": "The pot will be kept in a shared wooden box at the village dock. Each fisher will write the surplus weight on a communal ledger posted on the dock. At the end of the day, the village council will tally the total surplus and distribute it by dividing the weight equally among fishers who had a shortfall, giving each a share that brings them up to 1 unit. The fishery will keep a simple tally of each fisher's catch to determine who is short."
